00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:11.470
So she gets started talking about some of
the more interesting machine learning kinds

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:16.850
of things that people have been doing now
with all sorts of interesting data that out

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:17.850
there.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.680
And then I'll try to give a little bit more
perspective on the data management side.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:28.030
So if you're seeing our first slide, basically
it talks about technologies like databases,

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.400
data warehouses, and in today's talk, when
we talk about databases-- most of us are computer

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.760
scientists, as you heard-- we'll be talking
about a little bit more from the technical

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.180
meaning of databases rather than sort of any
community repository.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:48.280
So there's a generic use of the term database
that currently reference to any sort of managed

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.730
data, but we'll be a little bit more technical
about that.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.079
And in the process, we'll try to cover some
of the issues of structures, types and integrations.

00:02:55.079 --> 00:03:00.709
One way to think about what we will talk about
today is maybe-- at least in some aspects

00:03:00.709 --> 00:03:07.769
of it-- maybe more of a computer science view
of some of the issues that you heard Anita

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:10.700
talk about in the last presentation.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.280
So let's see.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.569
Yeah.

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:18.609
Hopefully, you're seeing our second slide.

00:03:18.609 --> 00:03:21.799
So that's the two of us, Elena and myself.

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:24.420
Going further, the outline.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:32.019
And then I will talk about some of the data
science task examples, the kind of things

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:33.250
we like doing.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.700
And what we decided during this presentation,
in one hour, we have to give you a very quick

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.390
tour of all the things going on.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:44.540
So I'm going to take a sort of historical
view of data management, where we started

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:45.870
and where we are.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.290
And when we get there, I'll say why we are
trying to do that.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:55.010
I'll talk about some of the current technologies,
and then in a way, where things are going

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.219
and some of the take-home messages.

00:03:58.219 --> 00:04:02.170
So let's get started with Elena, who's right
here.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:03.170
Hi.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:04.430
Good morning, everyone.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:08.500
So this is a lecture series on the fundamentals
of data science.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.890
I thought it would be helpful to give you
a data scientist perspective on why today's

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:17.709
topic is important in the process of doing
data science.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.910
And while you don't necessarily need to understand
all the technology details of the data management

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:26.790
solutions out there, as a data scientist,
you do need to have a good idea when to use

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.380
one solution versus another.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:34.700
So first, I will give you some typical scenarios
that data scientists face.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:39.590
Imagine that you're interested in one of these
three research questions listed on this slide.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.880
For example, how do new research topics emerge?

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.440
How do kids use playgrounds?

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.770
How can you create more secure code?

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.690
There are many different approaches that you
can take to answer these questions.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.410
But given that we live in the age of big data,
I will reformulate these questions specifically

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:03.860
to take advantage of large, publicly-available
datasets.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.110
So the first question was about how new research
topics emerge.

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:13.120
Network scientists may approach this problem
by studying citation networks.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:19.130
More specifically, which articles reference
which other articles, and how research topics

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:22.500
propagated through these articles over time.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.100
And as most of you know, one large data source
of publication citations is PubMed.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:34.370
PubMed currently contains over 26 million
articles, and probably an order of magnitude

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:42.070
more reference bearers, or which articles
cite which other articles.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:51.030
So as you can see, this is already a problem
that can be solved through a lot of data.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:57.090
The second research question that I presented
was one that a behavioral scientist might

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.360
be interested in, about how kids use playgrounds.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.320
And more specifically, the angle on this question
that I'm taking here is what do user-generated

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.490
videos on playgrounds tell us about how kids
use playgrounds?

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:20.530
So for example, in September this year, Google
released a data set for research purposes

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:27.620
from YouTube which contains 8 million videos
and around 1.5 terabytes of data.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:33.680
And each video is actually tagged by the entities
that are included in the different frames

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.150
of the video.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:37.120
And you can actually search by entity.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:42.810
So for example, if I search for playground,
I can find that this dataset contains 255

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.330
videos of playgrounds.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:47.330
That's great.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.900
255 videos is not a lot of data, right?

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.600
It's only 255.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.560
However, for you to extract these videos and
do an analysis on these videos, you need to

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:01.280
download the full 1.5 terabytes of data and
find these videos in order to do analysis

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.360
on them.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:11.650
The third research question that I presented
was about how to create more secure code.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.640
And it's a question that security researchers
might be interested in.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.710
The specific angle that we can take on it
is what you can learn from version updates

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:26.890
of code repositories in order to create more
secure code.

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.260
And there's an example here I would like to
give, Github.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:37.300
Github is very popular too amongst software
developers, and it currently contains over

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.940
38 million repositories.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:47.490
Moreover, Google actually made 2.8 million
of these repositories available for querying

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.150
through BigQuery.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.210
And this is already over 3 terabytes of data.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:57.360
And here, I'm just giving a specific example
of a few such repositories that are related

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.440
to computational biology.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.240
So all these three tasks actually require
processing large amounts of data.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.240
And today, we're not going to talk about how
we're going to answer these questions.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:16.150
But we will actually talk about how we can
process such data.

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:20.970
So when you answer research questions with
data, there are many different sources of

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.060
data that you may have access to.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:27.340
Some of them may have structured data, others
semi-structured data, but the majority of

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.950
data out there falls in the category of unstructured
data.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:34.950
Structured data is data that has a pretty
fine data model, such as data and relational

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:39.690
databases, and it is fairly straightforward
to query.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:43.640
Semi-structured data is a type of structured
data where your schema is often contained

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.310
within the data itself.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:50.060
And some examples include XML and JSON files.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:54.700
Unstructured data does not have a pre-defined
data model, which makes them harder to query.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:59.580
And some examples include scientific articles,
where you can have a mix of figures, algorithms,

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:04.130
and text, and where each article may have
different section structure.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:10.650
So one may start with introduction and then
go on to algorithms, and another one may start

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:15.410
with an introduction and then jump into actual
data analysis.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:22.800
So video data is another example where the
video frames do not follow this earlier pre-defined

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.050
data model.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:30.100
And lastly, code repositories can have different
folders and file structures and end types.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:37.250
Now that you know what kind of data you're
dealing with, you need to decide on where

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.400
you will store the data and
how you can query to answer your questions

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.390
of interest.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:46.630
All three tasks that I described require you
to process large amounts of data, and there

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:51.880
are many solutions to storing and managing
large datasets that exist out there.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:56.780
One can easily spend a lot of time exploring
and trying to decide on the best tool to use.

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:58.650
Do you use the latest tool?

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.760
Do you stick to using what you feel most comfortable
with?

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.740
And the answer is it depends.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.500
Some of these tools provide a similar set
of capabilities.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.130
Others are complementary to each other.

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:14.510
For example, if you're a software application
developer, you may store the data that your

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.080
application collects in a MySQL database.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:21.260
But you may want to export it do a Hadoop
class or a cloud service in order to enable

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.420
fast analytics on it.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.620
You may also want to use a Redis store for
the results from your analytics engine, so

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:34.870
that your application has fast access to your
analytical results.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:38.560
Something that plays an important role in
deciding on the best tools to use for a specific

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.750
task is the characteristics of the data that
you're dealing with.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:47.920
You need to take into consideration the volume
of the data, its variety and diversity, whether

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.880
it's streaming and at what speed, and also
what you plan to use the data for.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.810
Whether it's an internal report that you need
to generate once a day, or whether it is to

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:02.030
produce analytical data that has to be made
available real time.

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.960
And today, instead of making specific recommendations
on which tools you should use, or talking

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.318
about low-level details of the technology
behind them, we would like to take you on

00:11:08.318 --> 00:11:14.310
a journey in the history of databases and
how the different modern tools came to be

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.380
and why they're important.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:17.610
Great.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:20.060
Thanks, Elena.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:24.800
So I'm going to then switch into sort of the
history mode.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:31.100
And as I said, we sort of decided that we
might want to give you more of an overview

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.360
of the landscape of what's out there.

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:39.650
So if any of you are computer science people
or have been interacting with computer scientists,

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:45.620
especially with all the recent stuff going
on with big data and the whole focus on data,

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:49.450
you might have heard people talking about,
well, so what's new here?

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:54.174
Are we doing anything different, and how does
it relate to what we've already been doing?

00:11:54.174 --> 00:11:59.440
So we thought we'd give you a perspective
on where we are coming from, which hopefully,

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.180
we'll understand where we are going.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:05.600
So where we are today, and where we are going.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:11.770
I think the take-home message-- which I actually
have a site on there at the end-- but to give

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:17.600
you right up front is, we are now in a situation
where there are a lot of choices available

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:19.150
to us.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:20.150
That's good and bad.

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:23.330
I mean, it's bad in the sense that that means
you'll understand what your choices are and

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:28.150
make the right decisions, but it's good in
the sense that, depending on the kind of problem

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:33.290
you're tackling-- like you heard three different
examples-- you could actually choose different

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:34.380
solution methods.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:39.510
And again, as I'll say at the end, some of
it may depend on how much freedom you have,

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.870
and are you doing it for yourself, or are
you in a

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.810
corporate context where maybe you don't have
as much control.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.160
But anyway, let's launch into this.

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:58.170
One more thing I wanted to say before starting
is, I didn't actually spend a minute introducing

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.170
ourselves.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:05.500
So just to reiterate, Elena's a [INAUDIBLE]
fellow here at NSF, but from next year she's

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:09.770
going to be on the faculty at the University
of Illinois, Chicago.

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:14.530
I'm here at NSF, as you heard, but I'm actually
here from the University of California in

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:15.530
San Diego.

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:21.600
So I'm a UCSD person, and I have a couple
of references our UCSD projects.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:28.630
And as you heard last week from Anita Bandrowski,
who's also from UC San Diego.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:37.180
OK, so if you think back to way back when,
fundamentally, what people were doing was

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:41.020
writing programs dealing with files.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:46.320
And that actually takes us all the way back
to sort of the beginning of computing, so

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.750
the 1950s, 60s, where every time you thought
about a problem that you wanted to solve,

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:56.220
whether it's-- and by the way, another way
to think about the history also is, how many

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:58.780
people were doing this and where were they
doing it?

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:03.700
So in the early days, there actually were
very few people doing this kind of-- any kind

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:04.810
of computing.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:07.190
And most of it was happening in corporate
settings.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:13.520
So you can think about, in corporate settings,
people wanting to do inventory control, or

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:22.220
finance systems, or doing some kind computational--
something related to whether they were modeling

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:24.740
a physical process, or whatever.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:31.090
Every activity started with your having to
write, essentially, an [INAUDIBLE] program.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.670
And the program had to deal with its own data.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:42.720
Now, that quickly moved into a setting where
there 

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:47.300
was plenty of data, and you had to deal with
somehow making it more efficient.

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:54.010
So from literally flat files of data, people
invented new ways of getting more efficient

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:55.590
access to data.

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.760
That's what we call structured files.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:14:59.810
Oh, sorry.

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:01.400
There you go.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:09.730
And you move forward to the 60s and 70s, where
we have programming languages like EL1, COBOL,

00:15:09.730 --> 00:15:12.890
things that many of you may not have heard
about.

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:17.890
Actually, some of those are probably still
running in old systems.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:23.540
But basically, that was a world where you
were dealing with fairly slow hardware, and

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.190
disks were much slower than memory.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:30.310
Today, disks are still slow, but there's a
much, much more fine hierarchy of storage

00:15:30.310 --> 00:15:31.880
between disk and memory.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.020
In those days, it was pretty simple.

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:34.630
There was either a disk or a memory.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:40.430
And so you have to do something to bridge
across that.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:45.460
And so then we come to a situation where we
have, therefore, application logic.

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:49.200
So remember that anything that you wanted
to do with data, you would have to write a

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:50.450
different program, right?

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:56.190
So you have the so-called application logic
encoded in those programs, and those programs

00:15:56.190 --> 00:16:02.380
also hardwire the data that they are going
after.

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:11.470
And the kind of models that-- the situation
that people found themselves in was that multiple

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:16.470
different programs were creating the same
data over and over again.

00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:22.140
So you might have multiple applications in
a corporate context that are all dealing with

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.240
employees for one reason or the other.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:27.170
Maybe one is payroll, the other is benefits.

00:16:27.170 --> 00:16:30.339
And they were all recreating this employee
information.

00:16:30.339 --> 00:16:37.770
So the realization came that we're having
inconsistent data, and we are duplicating

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:38.770
data unnecessarily.

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:44.140
So that's sort of the start of thinking about
data as something separate from the application

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:45.320
that's working on the data.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:50.040
And that's a very important concept in computer
science, is the abstraction concept that begins

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:52.870
to separate application logic from the data.

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:57.480
You can think of it very easily, that there's
a database, I can go and write SQL queries

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.920
and get all sorts of ad-hoc information [INAUDIBLE]
database.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:07.010
That was not the case before, where the application
was very tightly tied to the kind of data

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:08.010
it was dealing with.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:12.249
And multiple applications created the same
data, because there was really no sharing

00:17:12.249 --> 00:17:13.249
going on.

00:17:13.249 --> 00:17:17.470
And the reason I'm harping on that is some
of this may come back to us, and some of the

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:19.149
present world might look like that.

00:17:19.149 --> 00:17:20.589
But you know, that's where we came from.

00:17:20.589 --> 00:17:25.289
And we realized that we should separate those
out.

00:17:25.289 --> 00:17:28.869
And there were these models called hierarchical
and network [INAUDIBLE] hierarchies that,

00:17:28.869 --> 00:17:31.970
most obviously, you can think about organizing
information.

00:17:31.970 --> 00:17:36.140
You have a corporation, you have departments,
you have people working in departments.

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:38.169
That sounds hierarchical.

00:17:38.169 --> 00:17:40.279
Or you have a human.

00:17:40.279 --> 00:17:46.190
Humans have different organs, and then you
can organize the human system as a bunch of

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:50.450
different organ systems, and that sounds like
a hierarchy.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:55.290
Well, soon, what happens is, you might say--
or think about courses, right?

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.919
So you have courses in a university.

00:17:58.919 --> 00:17:59.919
Courses have offerings.

00:17:59.919 --> 00:18:03.269
I offer a course this fall, next spring, et
cetera.

00:18:03.269 --> 00:18:04.860
Each offering has students.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:09.480
But what if you wanted to ask a question that's
says, for each student, tell me all the teachers

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.059
that are interacting with that student.

00:18:12.059 --> 00:18:15.299
That becomes a very difficult query to answer
if it's hierarchical in nature, because you

00:18:15.299 --> 00:18:21.059
have to sort of go up the hierarchy, find
out for each person who is the-- which course

00:18:21.059 --> 00:18:23.919
they're in, and then for that course, who's
the teacher.

00:18:23.919 --> 00:18:30.220
So when people found that there are inefficiencies
here, the network model was created, which

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:34.529
would say, well, maybe for these students,
I should just make pointers to all the teachers,

00:18:34.529 --> 00:18:38.340
if i'm asking this query very often, about
what are the set of teachers teaching the

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:42.100
student.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:48.289
But as you think about more and more such
queries that you want to do, the structure

00:18:48.289 --> 00:18:49.669
becomes very complicated.

00:18:49.669 --> 00:18:54.610
So that led to the need for people to think
about, what is a more abstract way in which

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:59.720
we can represent this information, so that
any query, more or less, could be equally

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:04.919
well answered, and not just stuffed with our
hardcoded into the database.

00:19:04.919 --> 00:19:10.340
And that leads to this model that we now have,
is the relational model, where we say, let's

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:13.710
store all information in tables.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:19.960
And there is some theory behind how we develop
these tables, and how we make sure that we

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.080
are not repeating information.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.919
So we remove replication.

00:19:24.919 --> 00:19:27.779
We increase the consistency of the database.

00:19:27.779 --> 00:19:31.220
And that's our relational databases.

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:35.340
And that's when we realize that the notion
of a database, now, in computer science, then,

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:40.759
is that there is something called a data model,
which is a formal definition of how data is

00:19:40.759 --> 00:19:41.759
represented.

00:19:41.759 --> 00:19:45.580
And then there is actually a formal query
language that we can build on top of it.

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:48.090
So relationally, this is a kind of set-oriented
data models.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.200
There are relations.

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:55.669
There are dependencies among columns in a
relation, and between relations.

00:19:55.669 --> 00:20:03.139
And then we can define the formal algebra,
which ends up being embodied in SQL.

00:20:03.139 --> 00:20:10.029
And also, by then, the kinds of things we
were doing with data were of two types.

00:20:10.029 --> 00:20:12.749
One was what you would call transactions.

00:20:12.749 --> 00:20:17.639
Again, this in a corporate context where data
is coming in, data is being updated.

00:20:17.639 --> 00:20:19.549
You might be doing purchases.

00:20:19.549 --> 00:20:21.029
You might be at a bank.

00:20:21.029 --> 00:20:22.769
You're doing bank transactions.

00:20:22.769 --> 00:20:29.109
So there's a whole set of technologies related
to transaction processing that are essentially

00:20:29.109 --> 00:20:33.139
doing insertions, deletions, updates on data.

00:20:33.139 --> 00:20:36.530
And then there's a whole set of things about
doing queries.

00:20:36.530 --> 00:20:38.790
And remember, in the old days, it was about
writing.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.970
For each query you wanted to do, you literally
had to write a program.

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:47.429
So the technology was, well, can we do common
technology that can support both?

00:20:47.429 --> 00:20:50.059
So transaction processing versus query processing.

00:20:50.059 --> 00:20:54.379
And I would say from around the 1980s onward
when the relational model came up, the idea

00:20:54.379 --> 00:21:01.720
was, let's use one system to do it all, and
which ended up being a success.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:06.899
Relational model, when it started, was not
viewed as that it might be successful.

00:21:06.899 --> 00:21:12.750
There were all sorts of issues, because it
was, in a way, slow to begin, compared to

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:13.750
existing technologies.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:17.690
But a lot of work was done to make it more
efficient.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:22.700
And as it became successful, it sort of became
the thing that everybody wanted to use to

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:23.779
store data.

00:21:23.779 --> 00:21:29.259
So then you got into these problems where
people said, as you heard earlier, that the

00:21:29.259 --> 00:21:30.649
data was getting more complex.

00:21:30.649 --> 00:21:36.720
People wanted to store more things, like images
or strings, or even videos.

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:42.299
So people wanted to store objects that were
more than what a standard database would support.

00:21:42.299 --> 00:21:50.059
Typical database might support your basic
data types, like integers.

00:21:50.059 --> 00:21:53.600
Oops, sorry.

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:57.139
I apologize.

00:21:57.139 --> 00:22:03.879
There's some interruption going here.

00:22:03.879 --> 00:22:06.109
OK.

00:22:06.109 --> 00:22:15.049
I think I'm back.

00:22:15.049 --> 00:22:19.360
So the question was, how do you support objects
in this?

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:25.659
And as we move to the 90s, we are also talking
about more object-oriented programming, so

00:22:25.659 --> 00:22:29.960
objects in C++ and Java.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:37.029
And the other was the need to support, say,
things like text, video, images.

00:22:37.029 --> 00:22:41.619
And this is the start of the first time we
started seeing this notion of variety.

00:22:41.619 --> 00:22:47.309
In this case, it's a variety of different
objects that are stored in the same database.

00:22:47.309 --> 00:22:51.690
And this is because the success by then of
the relational model meant that everybody

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:55.859
wanted to use that as the one stop shop for
data management.

00:22:55.859 --> 00:22:58.080
But then it starts putting stress on the technology,
right?

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:04.149
So as we move forward from there, we also
get to the place where we are putting more

00:23:04.149 --> 00:23:08.289
and more data in these systems, and more and
more people are using data.

00:23:08.289 --> 00:23:13.240
Remember, we started by saying there were
not that many people actually using computing.

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:16.159
And of course, by the 80s and 90s, this is
all over the place.

00:23:16.159 --> 00:23:21.620
So there's a lot more data that's being generated
that has to be managed, and the question was,

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.379
how do we build efficient systems to do that?

00:23:24.379 --> 00:23:28.799
So by the mid 90s, we had, so far, parallel
database systems.

00:23:28.799 --> 00:23:33.889
And these were still using the relational
model, still supporting SQL, which is very

00:23:33.889 --> 00:23:34.889
popular by then.

00:23:34.889 --> 00:23:42.169
By then providing ways of processing all that
in parallel, and there's a big sort of industry

00:23:42.169 --> 00:23:49.609
around that to support big clusters and parallel
computing on SQL databases.

00:23:49.609 --> 00:23:56.869
But then we come to a situation where, especially,
the internet web-scale companies like the

00:23:56.869 --> 00:24:02.359
Googles and Facebooks have arrived on the
scene, and they are seeing even more data.

00:24:02.359 --> 00:24:05.539
So this is even more volume from them.

00:24:05.539 --> 00:24:10.030
And actually, along with that also goes the
speed at which the data is arriving.

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:17.380
So there's also a problem of [INAUDIBLE] streams,
and a lot what we would call the velocity.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:23.139
And in a way, I don't know if you can call
it abandoned, but those companies realized

00:24:23.139 --> 00:24:28.710
that the relational technology that had built
up over the years, over the decades, was a

00:24:28.710 --> 00:24:32.039
little bit complicated to use for these extremely
large data.

00:24:32.039 --> 00:24:36.320
It does take a while to put your data in,
to structure it.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.840
And also, in these cases, the data was not
structured.

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:44.479
It had this sort of semi-structured characteristic
to it, because a lot of it was social media

00:24:44.479 --> 00:24:51.529
data coming in through websites or weblog
data which was not wellstructured.

00:24:51.529 --> 00:25:00.779
So that brings us systems like Hadoop, which
are massively parallel and very robust, for

00:25:00.779 --> 00:25:02.029
the level of parallelism they have.

00:25:02.029 --> 00:25:07.090
So literally, you're talking about thousands
of nodes of computers running, and there's

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:09.009
a robustness across it.

00:25:09.009 --> 00:25:15.720
Because in some sense, you can say it was
a very simple model that was implemented that

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:20.399
ensured that the overall system was very robust.

00:25:20.399 --> 00:25:26.580
And that's enhanced by the fact that hardware
was relatively cheap, and we had to really

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:32.299
literally deal with terabytes of data that
we are now seeing in many different domains.

00:25:32.299 --> 00:25:36.460
That also meant that the implementations had
to be simpler.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:42.159
You couldn't support the real complexity that
SQL had agglomerated by then.

00:25:42.159 --> 00:25:46.320
I mean, by this time, SQL is almost like a
full programming language.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:49.739
You couldn't really implement all of that
on massive terabyte systems.

00:25:49.739 --> 00:25:52.359
So all of a sudden, the language became simpler.

00:25:52.359 --> 00:25:59.669
And so you started hearing more things like
noSQL, which was meant to be simple SQL in

00:25:59.669 --> 00:26:02.610
the beginning, and now
people called it all sorts of different things,

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:07.240
including not only SQL, because the interesting
trend that's happening- that has happened

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:12.739
already in the Hadoop, these massively parallel
systems-- is SQL is coming back.

00:26:12.739 --> 00:26:16.440
People are now implementing more and more
of SQL on top of these systems.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:23.419
So this is where back to the future kind of
feeling comes back, and that's why we wanted

00:26:23.419 --> 00:26:26.960
to take you through this history as to try
and make sense of all the stuff that's going

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:27.960
on.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:31.190
So we started with simple query languages,
noSQL.

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:35.960
Because people realize that, even with those
terabyte systems, there are some very basic

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:41.690
things you should be able to do, like filtering
data out, doing simple aggregations-- what

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:51.299
in SQL you might call group by-making aggregates
on certain attribute values, maybe a date

00:26:51.299 --> 00:26:53.409
value, maybe the name of a person, whatever
it is.

00:26:53.409 --> 00:26:55.590
So simple aggregation is very important.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:00.399
And that brings you back into this world of
having some sort of query language on top.

00:27:00.399 --> 00:27:06.580
At the same time, there's all this essentially
unstructured stuff going on that Elena mentioned,

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:10.559
and things like JSON become very popular,
and all the web stuff becomes popular.

00:27:10.559 --> 00:27:14.190
So people wanted to take things that they
are creating in their programming languages

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:15.690
and dump it straight into database.

00:27:15.690 --> 00:27:20.590
So these systems have been required to support
things like JSON now as a data model.

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:25.970
So we have databases like Cassandra, and all
of those-- sorry, many of those-- and noSQL

00:27:25.970 --> 00:27:33.499
databases are going into that semi-structured
mode, and not necessarily the full relational

00:27:33.499 --> 00:27:34.499
model.

00:27:34.499 --> 00:27:36.179
Now, relational model has not gone away.

00:27:36.179 --> 00:27:37.940
So this is where your menu of options increase.

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:40.119
So you still have the relational model.

00:27:40.119 --> 00:27:44.179
You have pure Hadoop, if you want, really.

00:27:44.179 --> 00:27:47.960
Or you could have SQL on top of Hadoop, or
you could have JSON.

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:54.529
So this is where all the choices keep increasing.

00:27:54.529 --> 00:27:59.559
And finally, I think where we are right now
is, if we are looking at it from a corporate

00:27:59.559 --> 00:28:04.110
point of view, and also increasingly as individual
scientists, as we are solving more complex

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:07.919
problems, we want to deal with many sources
of data.

00:28:07.919 --> 00:28:14.019
It's not just necessarily-- there's clearly
a whole group of problems, including maybe

00:28:14.019 --> 00:28:18.830
in things like next generation sequencing
and so on, when there's one data type-- maybe

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:25.509
it's the genome sequence, or it's the social
media interaction graph-- that you're trying

00:28:25.509 --> 00:28:26.990
to mine and learn more from.

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:30.119
Or maybe it's just videos.

00:28:30.119 --> 00:28:33.749
But they're also now increasing-- we have
more problems where you want to put multiple

00:28:33.749 --> 00:28:34.749
different datasets together.

00:28:34.749 --> 00:28:38.849
Maybe you want to put census together with
electronic health records, et cetera.

00:28:38.849 --> 00:28:43.549
And that brings us to this world of very heterogeneous
data sources.

00:28:43.549 --> 00:28:47.149
And one concept that has now emerged is this
notion of a data lake.

00:28:47.149 --> 00:28:49.090
I'm going to say a little bit more about that.

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:53.320
But essentially, it's the idea that you can
dump all sorts of different data into one

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:54.320
place.

00:28:54.320 --> 00:28:58.239
It's loosely structured, and that's the sort
of analogy to a lake.

00:28:58.239 --> 00:29:02.070
But at least it's all in one place, and you
can do something with it.

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.480
So that's the full circle we've come around.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:09.139
Remember, we started in the 50s and 60s, and
here we are now.

00:29:09.139 --> 00:29:13.609
So we want to say
a little bit more about some of these concepts

00:29:13.609 --> 00:29:15.070
and where we might be going.

00:29:15.070 --> 00:29:19.220
So let's talk a little bit about this idea
of data warehouses versus data lakes.

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:28.110
So data warehouses started probably in the
late 80s to the 90s, when people had already

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:33.080
rationalized their data systems into relational
databases, or whatever, so they had reduced

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:34.499
the redundancy in data.

00:29:34.499 --> 00:29:38.960
But what they didn't realize is that there
were multiple different databases, even within

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:39.970
a large company.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:42.460
It could be geographic regions.

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:46.710
Maybe they had a division in Europe and a
division in the US, or the east coast and

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:48.889
west coast had their own data.

00:29:48.889 --> 00:29:53.769
Or the finance guys had one set of coherent
datasets, and the sales guys had a difference

00:29:53.769 --> 00:29:56.129
set of coherent datasets.

00:29:56.129 --> 00:30:02.480
And when people wanted to do decision support
inside such a system, they had to bring all

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:03.639
of these data together.

00:30:03.639 --> 00:30:07.269
So the data warehouse is really the concept
that, if you want to get a more holistic view

00:30:07.269 --> 00:30:11.840
of whatever it is that you're looking at,
you do have to bring data from different places

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:12.929
together.

00:30:12.929 --> 00:30:15.419
And that led to this creation of warehouses.

00:30:15.419 --> 00:30:18.989
So the data warehouses started at that time
when people said, oh, you know what?

00:30:18.989 --> 00:30:21.509
These data are not just about transactions.

00:30:21.509 --> 00:30:26.830
Another thing to remember is, a lot of the
roots of data management community actually

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:28.429
do come from industry.

00:30:28.429 --> 00:30:33.830
Because interestingly, that's where data started
initially, where the boom of data.

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:38.109
The science folks were doing their own little
thing, and they were not really using these

00:30:38.109 --> 00:30:39.109
technologies.

00:30:39.109 --> 00:30:43.759
And if you come back to today, this is also
where big data started, was actually back

00:30:43.759 --> 00:30:44.759
in industry.

00:30:44.759 --> 00:30:48.639
Because, again, they just had the imperative
to deal with the data, and they had to do

00:30:48.639 --> 00:30:50.009
something about it.

00:30:50.009 --> 00:30:55.049
So the data warehouses were the situation
where the business said, all of this data

00:30:55.049 --> 00:30:59.220
that we are collecting, can we do something
more with it in terms of messaging support

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:00.220
and strategy?

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:09.029
So what they realized when they started putting
that together is that there was some diversity

00:31:09.029 --> 00:31:13.999
in the way that the different databases were
being created, even if it was within one enterprise,

00:31:13.999 --> 00:31:16.249
one big company, or whatever.

00:31:16.249 --> 00:31:18.139
And that led to some heterogeneity.

00:31:18.139 --> 00:31:21.529
And so when you put all of this stuff back
together-- and let's say you're trying to

00:31:21.529 --> 00:31:27.039
get one common view of your customer, but
there may be a slightly different view of

00:31:27.039 --> 00:31:31.399
the customer in the finance database, and
a slightly different view in the sales database--

00:31:31.399 --> 00:31:35.409
this is where you have to start generating
metadata to say things like, OK, what we are

00:31:35.409 --> 00:31:41.519
calling a customer ID over there is something
else ID over here.

00:31:41.519 --> 00:31:49.859
So data warehouses are typically associated
with being heavily cleaned up in that way,

00:31:49.859 --> 00:31:52.239
having metadata.

00:31:52.239 --> 00:31:56.710
And there are a lot of rules about what can
get into a data warehouse and what doesn't

00:31:56.710 --> 00:32:02.289
get in, so that when you use it,
it's a very sort of a clean dataset.

00:32:02.289 --> 00:32:07.029
And if you've dealt with any data, you know
that cleaning up data is a big, big task,

00:32:07.029 --> 00:32:10.929
and you might have to make some difficult
decisions about what goes in and what doesn't

00:32:10.929 --> 00:32:12.409
go in.

00:32:12.409 --> 00:32:15.379
The flip side of it, the data lake is kind
of the opposite.

00:32:15.379 --> 00:32:18.090
You put everything in.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:24.830
You can imagine that structuring, cleaning,
and putting stuff into a data warehouse implies

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:29.330
that there is some time and effort involved,
and also implies that there may be some delay

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:30.330
involved.

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:33.629
That is, from the time that you actually received
the data-wherever you might have got it--

00:32:33.629 --> 00:32:37.659
in your operational system, till the time
it gets into the data warehouse, there's a

00:32:37.659 --> 00:32:38.659
delay.

00:32:38.659 --> 00:32:42.849
Therefore, when you're querying your warehouse,
you may not be getting the latest, greatest

00:32:42.849 --> 00:32:46.149
data that the corporation might have.

00:32:46.149 --> 00:32:50.070
So lakes were trying to solve that problem
to say, look, there are some applications

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:51.929
where I just need the latest data.

00:32:51.929 --> 00:32:53.999
Even if it's dirty, I'm going to deal with
it.

00:32:53.999 --> 00:33:00.109
So remember, you may be back to the situation
where you might be writing individual applications

00:33:00.109 --> 00:33:04.159
that are working with the data lake, but you're
having to do it just because you want to get

00:33:04.159 --> 00:33:09.489
the current status, and you're not happy that's
something that's one quarter old, or even

00:33:09.489 --> 00:33:12.210
a month old, or even maybe a week old.

00:33:12.210 --> 00:33:14.840
So that's kind of the difference between these
two environments.

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:21.989
Data warehouses are [INAUDIBLE] a single consistent
store of data, but they require you to transform

00:33:21.989 --> 00:33:24.109
and ingest the data into a warehouse.

00:33:24.109 --> 00:33:27.139
There's a notion of a star schema.

00:33:27.139 --> 00:33:28.730
OK, let's see.

00:33:28.730 --> 00:33:33.769
For this image, it shows that you're getting
data from different sources.

00:33:33.769 --> 00:33:37.499
There is some step of so-called integration
you have to do, which is all the cleaning

00:33:37.499 --> 00:33:40.830
up and the rationalization, before you can
put it into a warehouse.

00:33:40.830 --> 00:33:45.600
So there is work involved there, but then
it enables a lot of different applications.

00:33:45.600 --> 00:33:51.659
The notion of a schema is-- the reason you
create data warehouses like this is in order

00:33:51.659 --> 00:33:52.970
to get this holistic view.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:56.559
It's the holistic view of some entity that
you care about.

00:33:56.559 --> 00:34:01.269
So typically, it could be a customer, if you're
a company, or it could be a patient.

00:34:01.269 --> 00:34:07.539
It could be an individual, if you are dealing
with health care or public health, right?

00:34:07.539 --> 00:34:11.889
So that thing that you care about is sort
of referred to as the fact, that fact has

00:34:11.889 --> 00:34:13.980
many dimensions associated with it.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:16.360
So that's this idea of a star.

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:23.639
So for example, we might care about a person
and a person's health and wellbeing.

00:34:23.639 --> 00:34:28.929
And a person might have different dimensions
like their sickness record, which is essentially

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:30.389
the electronic health record.

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:32.980
Every time they got sick, there is some entry
in the EHR.

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:38.010
But we could also have something about their
activity, their food habit, where they have

00:34:38.010 --> 00:34:40.800
they traveled, what's their family information.

00:34:40.800 --> 00:34:42.589
So those would all be the dimensions.

00:34:42.589 --> 00:34:48.190
And to push on that analogy a little further,
there is also this notion of a snowflake schema.

00:34:48.190 --> 00:34:53.149
That is, each of those dimensions itself might
have a little bit of a structure.

00:34:53.149 --> 00:34:58.480
But these are the kind of things that we deal
with when we talk about a data warehouse.

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:03.050
I won't look too much into this slide, but
it sort of repeats what I said before.

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:05.859
There are governance issues involved.

00:35:05.859 --> 00:35:11.650
Governance, by the way, is a word that's coming
back today in today's sort of data-driven

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:17.529
world, as people realize that they really
want to use data to make decisions, whether

00:35:17.529 --> 00:35:24.580
it's a serious scientific discovery or a corporation
making some strategic decisions.

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:27.940
And governance, then, is important, because
you need to know things like provenance.

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:30.420
You need to know, where did this data come
from?

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:33.400
What did we do with this data?

00:35:33.400 --> 00:35:40.920
So just as an aside, the federal government
has started something called the-- it's actually

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:46.450
called the Federal Data Cabinet, but it's
a collection of all the chief data officers

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:48.549
across federal agencies.

00:35:48.549 --> 00:35:51.730
And now there are about 125 or so people in
that group.

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:56.750
And I sit in on that just representing NSF,
and one of the first things-- first groups--

00:35:56.750 --> 00:36:01.050
they started was a group that was going to
look at governance.

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:04.349
And [INAUDIBLE] your first step in governance
is to figure out, what are the data assets

00:36:04.349 --> 00:36:05.349
that we have?

00:36:05.349 --> 00:36:09.130
And your next step is to say, well, what can
we say about the quality of these assets,

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:10.130
right?

00:36:10.130 --> 00:36:16.119
So data governance is an important thing,
and warehouses really do focus a lot on that.

00:36:16.119 --> 00:36:19.410
But they are not good for unstructured data.

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:23.980
There is latency between when the data comes
into an organization and when it gets into

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:28.099
a data warehouse, for all the reasons we just
mentioned.

00:36:28.099 --> 00:36:30.970
So data lakes, in a sense, are trying to address
that.

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:33.520
And data lake is a new-- relatively new--
concept.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:35.789
It's a relatively new thing.

00:36:35.789 --> 00:36:39.630
Depending on who you talk to, people will
either say, well, that's a very important

00:36:39.630 --> 00:36:45.369
thing, or others will say, this is a waste
of time, or we're just revisiting other stuff.

00:36:45.369 --> 00:36:51.640
And there's now the word data swamp, which
refers to, if you put all sorts of data into

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:53.329
one place, is it really useful?

00:36:53.329 --> 00:36:55.690
We are just creating a swamp of data.

00:36:55.690 --> 00:36:57.609
So there are all these issues.

00:36:57.609 --> 00:37:02.319
But the problem is really-- and by the way,
and a lot of these data lakes are built on

00:37:02.319 --> 00:37:06.360
top of this Hadoop technology, which is a
highly scaleable technology.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:11.400
Because the point of data lakes is you want
to throw all your data into it, and you want

00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:13.480
to start doing some easy analytics.

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:17.830
And as you heard before, a lot of the analytics
may be going off to unstructured data.

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:20.020
They may not be in relational databases.

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:25.050
You may actually be writing a program or a
script of some sort that wants to deal with

00:37:25.050 --> 00:37:29.059
sort of relatively unstructured flat files,
really.

00:37:29.059 --> 00:37:34.349
And so it's back to the flat file world, and
the lakes support that.

00:37:34.349 --> 00:37:43.260
OK, so data lakes are increasingly also going
towards this notion of some structuring of

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:45.760
data within the lake.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:52.619
And some of the ideas you will see that corporations
are embracing there is this notion of a [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:52.619 --> 00:38:01.730
So your data may come and land in some landing
zone where it's all non-clean, whatever data

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:02.730
you've collected.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:07.890
And there may be a sequence of steps that
it goes through, and that creates this sort

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.480
of workflow process.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:13.160
That's actually a useful thing, because then
you might have different applications that

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:16.880
might say, you know what, I'm willing to go
after the dirty, dangerous data.

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:21.140
Or another location might say, I'm going to
go after data in stage three, after the screening

00:38:21.140 --> 00:38:22.140
has been done.

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:28.039
So at least in creates the opportunity, rather
than having to say, well, you can't do anything

00:38:28.039 --> 00:38:30.099
until all the data is cleaned up.

00:38:30.099 --> 00:38:36.200
So there are some positives to it, but you
have to be a little bit careful in how you're

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:40.279
creating these kinds of assets.

00:38:40.279 --> 00:38:42.440
So now let me flip to a project.

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:49.260
Turns out it's a project that UC San Diego
that I've been involved in, and not so much

00:38:49.260 --> 00:38:53.869
involved in the last couple of years, since
I've come to NSF.

00:38:53.869 --> 00:38:59.760
It's actually led by our fearless leader there,
Kevin Patrick, who is in population and public

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:02.480
health at UC San Diego.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:06.630
There are other-- so almost half the team
is from the med school.

00:39:06.630 --> 00:39:12.710
They have Ted Chan, who actually heads the
Beacon Project in San Diego, for those of

00:39:12.710 --> 00:39:14.260
you know what the Beacon initiative.

00:39:14.260 --> 00:39:19.950
It's just trying to integrate data from all
sorts of different DHR and other systems.

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:23.180
[INAUDIBLE] is our bioinformatics person.

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:27.349
But the other half of the project is computer
scientists.

00:39:27.349 --> 00:39:31.460
So Yannis and Sanjoy and so on are professors
in computer science.

00:39:31.460 --> 00:39:38.440
And the idea that we had here was, in a sense,
what warehouses and lakes are trying to do.

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:45.609
That is, in order to look at the whole information
related to an individual, the whole of the

00:39:45.609 --> 00:39:50.309
health information, you need to put together
data from different sources.

00:39:50.309 --> 00:39:54.789
And of course, there are many projects that
have been doing it, are doing it, and will

00:39:54.789 --> 00:39:55.789
continue to do it.

00:39:55.789 --> 00:40:00.720
But our idea in this project was, can we try
to standardize some of this, so that we don't

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:03.430
have to repeat the same thing again and again?

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:07.079
A lot of people are trying to do the same
thing, and trying to put together, maybe,

00:40:07.079 --> 00:40:12.640
activity data with DHR data with pollution
data.

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:17.869
Let's try to see if we can make common APIs,
common processes, so that others don't have

00:40:17.869 --> 00:40:19.260
to repeat that.

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:25.650
So that's basically the idea, and we came
up with this notion of a whole health information

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:27.549
model, and an API facilitated that.

00:40:27.549 --> 00:40:32.609
And I just wanted to mention that because
this is a classic example of trying to integrate

00:40:32.609 --> 00:40:33.750
very heterogeneous data.

00:40:33.750 --> 00:40:37.099
It has semi-structured sources in there.

00:40:37.099 --> 00:40:38.940
It has structured sources in there.

00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:44.470
I don't think, initially, we were going after
any text or unstructured or even video, but

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:46.930
you know, that might be one of the things
we might put in there.

00:40:46.930 --> 00:40:52.170
What it's trying to do is integrate information
from very different layers.

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:59.799
So as Kevin likes to point out, it's not about
just integrating information inside a hospital

00:40:59.799 --> 00:41:01.580
or inside a clinical setting.

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:06.470
It's actually about integrating all the health
information related to a person, some of which

00:41:06.470 --> 00:41:11.560
may from a hospital, but a lot of it has to
do with behavioral and environmental.

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:16.880
And if you really think in terms of the whole
health, that, in some ways, could be a lot

00:41:16.880 --> 00:41:22.650
more important than just your disease and
sickness record.

00:41:22.650 --> 00:41:24.990
So anyway, that's sort of the goal of that
project.

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:30.059
And so, flipping back to the kinds of data
that we'll encounter, as Elena said before,

00:41:30.059 --> 00:41:33.950
we are not going to get into the details,
but we are trying to give you sort of a quick

00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:35.349
survey of things.

00:41:35.349 --> 00:41:39.190
So coming back to this notion of structured,
semi-structured, and so-called unstructured,

00:41:39.190 --> 00:41:41.730
what do we mean by that?

00:41:41.730 --> 00:41:44.779
From a computer science point of view, I said
that earlier.

00:41:44.779 --> 00:41:49.910
Already we think of a database as something
that's structured, as the notion of a data

00:41:49.910 --> 00:41:55.010
model is a formal specification of the data
and a query language that allows you to query

00:41:55.010 --> 00:41:56.010
that.

00:41:56.010 --> 00:42:00.250
Actually, another important thing that relational
databases do have is this notion of integrating

00:42:00.250 --> 00:42:01.250
constraints.

00:42:01.250 --> 00:42:06.029
And they are very important in order to make
sure that your data is clean.

00:42:06.029 --> 00:42:12.529
So a simple integrated constraint could be
that Social Security number is a unique number.

00:42:12.529 --> 00:42:16.270
You can't have two people with the same Social
Security number.

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:21.460
Another constraint could be that, if somebody
is working in a department, the name of that

00:42:21.460 --> 00:42:22.829
department should exist, right?

00:42:22.829 --> 00:42:26.010
So you couldn't be working in a department
that doesn't exist.

00:42:26.010 --> 00:42:34.130
So that led to the [INAUDIBLE] characteristics
that are associated with structured databases,

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:38.750
referred to as ACID, [INAUDIBLE], Consistency,
Isolation, Durability.

00:42:38.750 --> 00:42:42.680
These are all essentially transaction properties
that make sure that everything you do with

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:49.319
the database results in a correct state of
the database, that you can't do transactions

00:42:49.319 --> 00:42:51.710
and queries on a database and mess up the
database.

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:56.030
And so the database ensures that that doesn't
happen.

00:42:56.030 --> 00:42:59.619
We won't go into the details of each of those
technical things, but that's an important

00:42:59.619 --> 00:43:00.619
concept.

00:43:00.619 --> 00:43:05.460
Because we are back to this sort of [INAUDIBLE]
wireless world, as we've said before, with

00:43:05.460 --> 00:43:10.349
noSQL, JSON, some of which will have this
notion of relaxed consistency-- you might

00:43:10.349 --> 00:43:16.670
see the term-- where it's not necessarily
always true that the database is consistent,

00:43:16.670 --> 00:43:21.420
and therefore, applications have to be very
careful that they may be encountering data

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:23.200
that's not completely clean.

00:43:23.200 --> 00:43:28.329
And again, sometimes the reason they relax
those constraints is in order to be able to

00:43:28.329 --> 00:43:31.740
deal with either the volume of the data or
the rate of the data.

00:43:31.740 --> 00:43:35.210
If you're dealing with data that's coming
in very fast, you may not just have enough

00:43:35.210 --> 00:43:39.150
time to make sure that it's all consistent,
and so on.

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:42.891
So it's useful to know that we're back to
the world where we're breaking some of the

00:43:42.891 --> 00:43:46.160
rules that we had before.

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:50.589
So in structured databases, everything looks
very regular, like in a relational database.

00:43:50.589 --> 00:43:53.410
All rows have the same format, et cetera.

00:43:53.410 --> 00:44:00.049
Now, semi-structured databases-- as was mentioned,
an example would be XML-- they allow for a

00:44:00.049 --> 00:44:02.910
little bit-- actually, for ad hoc structures.

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:10.720
So if you think about a bibliography at the
end of an article, it would have all sorts

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:15.299
of things in it, like papers, books, all sorts
of the other things.

00:44:15.299 --> 00:44:19.839
And each of those might have a slightly different
structure, and you may not want to bend over

00:44:19.839 --> 00:44:22.420
backwards and try
to put all the data into a relational database.

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:25.269
A semi-structured model makes a lot of sense
there.

00:44:25.269 --> 00:44:28.549
A lot of web pages have that kind of structure,
right?

00:44:28.549 --> 00:44:35.260
Even if the content in the web page nowadays
may come from a database of some sort, the

00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:38.880
[INAUDIBLE] structured itself maybe better
represented as a semi-structured.

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:44.579
So I won't go into the syntax details, et
cetera.

00:44:44.579 --> 00:44:51.309
Just quickly, most of the trends where we
are going, it turns out, as we represent more

00:44:51.309 --> 00:44:57.220
and more complex information, whether it's,
say, cellular interactions or human interactions,

00:44:57.220 --> 00:45:01.020
graphs are a very important type of data.

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:05.490
And now on the technology side, people are
beginning to build systems that efficiently

00:45:05.490 --> 00:45:07.329
support graph data.

00:45:07.329 --> 00:45:11.060
So one of the things we do with graph data--
it's still useful to be able to query a graph,

00:45:11.060 --> 00:45:12.060
right?

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:15.190
So I can say, get me all the nodes in this
graph that have certain properties.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:18.579
But other things you do with graphs are traversing
the graph.

00:45:18.579 --> 00:45:24.710
We might want to find the shortest path between
x and y, and the path might represent some

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:28.150
connections between cells in the brain.

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:32.261
[INAUDIBLE] you heard about the neuroscience
information facility last time.

00:45:32.261 --> 00:45:34.010
They do a lot of those kinds of things.

00:45:34.010 --> 00:45:40.359
Graphs may also represent semantic linkages
for [INAUDIBLE] ontologies between terms.

00:45:40.359 --> 00:45:46.829
So there are a lot of cases where graphs turn
up, and graph data is increasingly a very

00:45:46.829 --> 00:45:52.240
important kind of data, might end up dominating
big parts of some of the applications.

00:45:52.240 --> 00:45:56.539
That's the graph of, actually, my LinkedIn
network.

00:45:56.539 --> 00:46:01.319
And we're also moving towards this notion
of creating knowledge graphs so that we can

00:46:01.319 --> 00:46:06.010
build in the semantics and understand the
meanings of the terms that we are referring

00:46:06.010 --> 00:46:07.619
to and the relationships between them.

00:46:07.619 --> 00:46:11.810
And I think you have heard about these concepts
in some of the other talks, and probably will

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:12.810
hear more.

00:46:12.810 --> 00:46:17.430
This is clearly-- there is something called
Semantic PubMed, where people are trying to

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:22.349
extract the semantics of the articles and
make those connections between articles based

00:46:22.349 --> 00:46:28.140
on the meaning of the stuff, rather than just
the specific words.

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:33.390
Another trend that's rapidly approaching--
and my own view is, in future, we'll think

00:46:33.390 --> 00:46:37.570
of everything as a stream, except some streams
will be much slower than others-- is data

00:46:37.570 --> 00:46:38.570
streams.

00:46:38.570 --> 00:46:42.299
And that's because the rate at which data
is coming at us, regardless of what the source

00:46:42.299 --> 00:46:45.720
of data is, is just growing more and more.

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:48.800
And so everything starts looking like just
a sequence of stuff.

00:46:48.800 --> 00:46:54.180
And examples right now are things like log
files on a computer, e-commerce purchases,

00:46:54.180 --> 00:47:00.500
what people call click streams, when you're
clicking on a website, game information, information

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:01.500
from social networks.

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:05.319
Twitter tweets are all streamed, and so on.

00:47:05.319 --> 00:47:10.849
And so there's a lot of this going on, and
there are new technologies that are being

00:47:10.849 --> 00:47:13.089
developed.

00:47:13.089 --> 00:47:18.350
One of the ones-- this is just one example--
is from Amazon, called Kinesis, that allows

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:20.960
you to sort of capture the stream of stuff
coming in.

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:23.319
And here, the example is mobile phones.

00:47:23.319 --> 00:47:26.490
But the project that I've been working on
with

00:47:26.490 --> 00:47:31.250
Kevin Patrick-- actually, there are a couple
of other projects that I didn't mention involving

00:47:31.250 --> 00:47:32.250
M.D.

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:37.240
Anderson Cancer Center-- is where we are putting
sensors on people, whether it's smartphones

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:43.119
or other kinds of sensors, and measuring various
body parameters.

00:47:43.119 --> 00:47:46.869
And those are all coming in as streams, and
there's going to be more and more of that,

00:47:46.869 --> 00:47:48.890
as I'm sure you're all familiar with.

00:47:48.890 --> 00:47:52.240
And then at the end of it, that data would
go into all sorts of different storage.

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:58.400
And that sort of S3, Redshift, Amazon Elastic
Search are all, again, different ways of storing

00:47:58.400 --> 00:47:59.400
this.

00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:04.660
So like we said before, your menu of options
are many right now.

00:48:04.660 --> 00:48:07.049
So finally some take-home messages.

00:48:07.049 --> 00:48:12.819
So when you're looking at the data, each of
us as individuals might be focused on one

00:48:12.819 --> 00:48:16.299
aspect of it, but really, every piece of data
has a lifecycle.

00:48:16.299 --> 00:48:18.099
And increasingly, we have to worry about it.

00:48:18.099 --> 00:48:23.200
How was it collected, where did it come from,
and where is it going to end up?

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:28.319
And people are very concerned nowadays, and
especially in academia, we think of a paper

00:48:28.319 --> 00:48:30.119
as the ultimate goal.

00:48:30.119 --> 00:48:35.640
The ultimate goal really should be the data
that's related with the paper, and where does

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:36.640
the data end up?

00:48:36.640 --> 00:48:41.650
So until that data has ended up somewhere
safely, we shouldn't say that your work is

00:48:41.650 --> 00:48:42.650
done.

00:48:42.650 --> 00:48:44.359
By just publishing a paper, it shouldn't be
the end of it.

00:48:44.359 --> 00:48:45.359
Performance.

00:48:45.359 --> 00:48:49.180
I mean, speaking as computer people, and I
know that Anita mentioned something about

00:48:49.180 --> 00:48:52.269
this last time, that computer scientists care
a lot about performance.

00:48:52.269 --> 00:48:54.680
Yes, we do, making things go fast.

00:48:54.680 --> 00:48:56.640
That's what I mean.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:48:59.820
And actually, going fast can be a game changer,
right?

00:48:59.820 --> 00:49:00.820
Just think about it.

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:06.089
If every little data step that you had to
do took whatever, that will completely change

00:49:06.089 --> 00:49:07.549
how you do your data analysis.

00:49:07.549 --> 00:49:10.359
If things go very fast, it's a big enabler.

00:49:10.359 --> 00:49:16.670
So computer science is always focused on how
to make it work faster and faster.

00:49:16.670 --> 00:49:17.970
Other take-home messages.

00:49:17.970 --> 00:49:23.339
In the world of data science, there are a
lot of different problems, and it really goes

00:49:23.339 --> 00:49:28.350
back to the lifecycle question, everything
from cleaning to prediction.

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:32.390
And there are all these tools you can use
along the way.

00:49:32.390 --> 00:49:36.650
So increasingly, you're in an environment
where you can actually use the right data

00:49:36.650 --> 00:49:38.580
management tool for the right job.

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:44.259
It just means you need to have some awareness
of what is available, but also what might

00:49:44.259 --> 00:49:48.009
you be giving up, if you pick one option versus
the other?

00:49:48.009 --> 00:49:49.009
So what does it buy you?

00:49:49.009 --> 00:49:52.400
And then finally, of course, there is the
context issue.

00:49:52.400 --> 00:49:55.029
Is it something you're just doing by yourself?

00:49:55.029 --> 00:50:01.829
You're doing it in a lab, in an institution
context, maybe a community, which may either

00:50:01.829 --> 00:50:05.279
give you more options or may restrict what
you can do.

00:50:05.279 --> 00:50:08.890
So that's just sort of the big picture.

00:50:08.890 --> 00:50:15.549
The last thing I wanted to say is something
that we've been looking at here in the world

00:50:15.549 --> 00:50:16.549
of science.

00:50:16.549 --> 00:50:20.351
So if you think about the agenda of data as
it's going to grow into the future, data is

00:50:20.351 --> 00:50:25.710
going to be more and more important to us
everywhere in our lives, right?

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:30.780
And there's going to be a lot of decisions,
whether we make them or some automated device

00:50:30.780 --> 00:50:32.849
or a robot makes them.

00:50:32.849 --> 00:50:34.950
It will all be data-driven.

00:50:34.950 --> 00:50:39.260
So here, I'm starting with an example of just
computer science, our own discipline.

00:50:39.260 --> 00:50:45.920
In computer science itself, there are areas
in CS that are becoming more data-intensive.

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:49.779
So there's more and more data available to
do research in computer science.

00:50:49.779 --> 00:50:51.369
An example would be internet traffic.

00:50:51.369 --> 00:50:56.730
There's a whole network research community
in computer science that researches internet

00:50:56.730 --> 00:51:01.270
traffic and tries to understand what's going
on on the internet by looking at that traffic.

00:51:01.270 --> 00:51:05.839
The amount of traffic is just tremendously
growing, obviously.

00:51:05.839 --> 00:51:07.349
Another one is data center logs.

00:51:07.349 --> 00:51:12.069
Increasingly, we have these massive data centers
with lots of computers, and you can actually

00:51:12.069 --> 00:51:16.549
look at the logs that have all sorts of information
about the performance, the temperature, et

00:51:16.549 --> 00:51:17.549
cetera.

00:51:17.549 --> 00:51:22.210
And actually, you can use that to optimize
energy consumption and all sorts of other

00:51:22.210 --> 00:51:23.210
things.

00:51:23.210 --> 00:51:29.349
So there is an example just in computer science
of data coming at us and us having to deal

00:51:29.349 --> 00:51:32.830
with it to do better computer science research.

00:51:32.830 --> 00:51:38.089
Now, we happen to think that data science--
and actually engineering, which is a very

00:51:38.089 --> 00:51:42.829
important part related to data-- is a thing
unto itself.

00:51:42.829 --> 00:51:48.269
I mean, it's almost like a discipline to itself,
is data science and engineering, which has

00:51:48.269 --> 00:51:49.470
its own theories.

00:51:49.470 --> 00:51:54.369
And [INAUDIBLE], for example, just released
a solicitation for developing the principles

00:51:54.369 --> 00:51:56.250
of data science.

00:51:56.250 --> 00:52:01.000
And there are issues like data representation,
and we mentioned some of that today.

00:52:01.000 --> 00:52:06.160
Modeling, whether statistical modeling or
representation modeling, machine learning,

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:11.579
decision support, reproducibility, I think
all of those together from the sort of discipline

00:52:11.579 --> 00:52:13.349
of data science and engineering.

00:52:13.349 --> 00:52:19.549
And our idea is that there is a translational
aspect, which is the aspect of taking all

00:52:19.549 --> 00:52:25.730
these principles and theories of data science
and applying it to real-world problems.

00:52:25.730 --> 00:52:30.460
And that application, there is some analogy
to this notion of translational medicine,

00:52:30.460 --> 00:52:34.490
where you're taking about taking research
results and applying it in a clinical context.

00:52:34.490 --> 00:52:40.839
Really, what all of us-- many of us, at least--
do with data is its clinical context of data,

00:52:40.839 --> 00:52:41.839
right?

00:52:41.839 --> 00:52:45.009
We are using data in the real world, and we
are thinking what techniques should I use

00:52:45.009 --> 00:52:46.079
on that data?

00:52:46.079 --> 00:52:47.869
That's translational stuff.

00:52:47.869 --> 00:52:51.540
And so there are obviously data-intensive
everything going on.

00:52:51.540 --> 00:52:54.119
There's biology, geoscience, medicine, and
so on.

00:52:54.119 --> 00:53:02.539
So we see an opportunity here to think about--
a lot of what's actually being done there

00:53:02.539 --> 00:53:08.019
is really translational work, and we are trying
to pursue that idea little bit further here

00:53:08.019 --> 00:53:09.049
at NSF.

00:53:09.049 --> 00:53:11.250
And I think that's it.

00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:14.369
There's a few more minutes for questions.

00:53:14.369 --> 00:53:15.369
Great.

00:53:15.369 --> 00:53:18.230
Thank you so much, Drs.

00:53:18.230 --> 00:53:19.230
Baru and Zheleva.

00:53:19.230 --> 00:53:21.880
That was a great talk.

00:53:21.880 --> 00:53:26.900
So we have one question from the audience
at this point, which is about the data lake

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:28.160
and the data warehouse.

00:53:28.160 --> 00:53:34.010
So the question is, can data be put into the
data lake and into the data warehouse at the

00:53:34.010 --> 00:53:36.930
same time as it's being prepared for the data
warehouse?

00:53:36.930 --> 00:53:41.329
And could you comment a little bit about potential
parallel sort of framework?

00:53:41.329 --> 00:53:42.819
Yes.

00:53:42.819 --> 00:53:45.230
That's a good question.

00:53:45.230 --> 00:53:50.240
And in a sense, the answer, I guess, is a
qualified yes, only in the sense that, like

00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:53.650
I said, the data warehouse is definitely very
prescriptive.

00:53:53.650 --> 00:54:00.579
Things can't go into the data warehouse unless
they meet a certain set of conditions.

00:54:00.579 --> 00:54:03.730
That's sort of the notion of a warehouse.

00:54:03.730 --> 00:54:07.400
As we said, a lake, you could more or less
dump anything in there.

00:54:07.400 --> 00:54:12.250
But we also said that in a data lake, people
are now beginning to actually talk about phases

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:13.250
of the data.

00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:17.000
So the data could go through multiple phases
of structuring and cleaning.

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:23.920
So it could be that at some point in that
process, the data has reached a level of cleanliness,

00:54:23.920 --> 00:54:28.269
let's say, that your data warehouse would
say, OK, I can accept that.

00:54:28.269 --> 00:54:29.369
And then you would do it.

00:54:29.369 --> 00:54:36.099
And the reason to do it is-- data warehousing,
by the way, is a big industry, probably multiple

00:54:36.099 --> 00:54:37.099
billion dollars.

00:54:37.099 --> 00:54:43.970
So there are a lot of good tools, querying
tools, analysis tools, as well as visualization

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:44.970
tools for your data.

00:54:44.970 --> 00:54:49.400
So you may want to actually use all of those
things, whereas data lake is still a little

00:54:49.400 --> 00:54:51.570
bit out there in the wild west right now.

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:54.849
So that might be a reason why you want to
do both, yeah.

00:54:54.849 --> 00:54:55.849
OK.

00:54:55.849 --> 00:55:01.760
So another question is, often time when we're
designing these types of databases, the queries

00:55:01.760 --> 00:55:04.579
themselves are not necessarily known ahead
of time.

00:55:04.579 --> 00:55:10.390
And often, we want to design these databases
in order to optimize the query response.

00:55:10.390 --> 00:55:17.099
But without knowing the queries, or if we're
trying to support sort of an exploratory set

00:55:17.099 --> 00:55:22.759
of data and queries in that nature, it's very
difficult to know ahead of time which type

00:55:22.759 --> 00:55:27.540
of database we should be using, what type
of data schema we should be using.

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:32.079
Are there any approaches, for instance, that
take a hybrid approach, where data actually

00:55:32.079 --> 00:55:37.039
goes into multiple different types of databases
simultaneously?

00:55:37.039 --> 00:55:38.710
Actually another excellent question.

00:55:38.710 --> 00:55:45.450
OK, so the first answer I want to give you
there, actually, is something I didn't cover.

00:55:45.450 --> 00:55:46.450
Maybe I should have.

00:55:46.450 --> 00:55:47.450
Maybe we can do it in the future.

00:55:47.450 --> 00:55:48.450
It's this notion of polystores.

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:52.609
It's still, I would say, more in the computer
science community.

00:55:52.609 --> 00:55:56.640
So poly as in P-O-L-Y store.

00:55:56.640 --> 00:56:02.369
And polystore idea is exactly what you're
saying, where people are realizing that, here's

00:56:02.369 --> 00:56:08.789
an application that I'm interested in, but
this application has different kinds of data

00:56:08.789 --> 00:56:10.970
that are all related to that application.

00:56:10.970 --> 00:56:15.349
And the different kind could mean some of
it is structured, [INAUDIBLE] structured.

00:56:15.349 --> 00:56:19.220
Other of it is, I'm not quite sure what the
structure is.

00:56:19.220 --> 00:56:23.049
But another piece of it may be a graph I actually
derive.

00:56:23.049 --> 00:56:24.049
So imagine this situation.

00:56:24.049 --> 00:56:31.150
I might have text, and I might process the
text to derive a graph out of it.

00:56:31.150 --> 00:56:35.810
Maybe it's a graph of all the people in that
text and the connections among the people.

00:56:35.810 --> 00:56:37.840
But I want to keep both.

00:56:37.840 --> 00:56:41.859
I want to keep the text of it, but I also
want to keep derived graph of it.

00:56:41.859 --> 00:56:44.220
So the graph might actually go into a graph
database.

00:56:44.220 --> 00:56:48.930
The text might just stay in some system that
does indexing of the text, and you might also

00:56:48.930 --> 00:56:50.019
have structured.

00:56:50.019 --> 00:56:55.890
So in computer science, and there are some
startup companies I'm aware of that are now

00:56:55.890 --> 00:57:02.990
beginning to do this kind of thing, saying,
let's invent a polystore and, in a way, try

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:08.109
to take the responsibility from the user and
put it in the system.

00:57:08.109 --> 00:57:10.150
So right now, I don't think that part is there
yet.

00:57:10.150 --> 00:57:13.700
Right now, the user still has to say, well,
this is a graph, and that's the text, and

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:15.950
this is that, and I want you to manage all
of these.

00:57:15.950 --> 00:57:22.079
But people are also looking at things like,
maybe the same data could be in multiple representations.

00:57:22.079 --> 00:57:27.800
And also, maybe the system can decide at what
point can it go from one to the other.

00:57:27.800 --> 00:57:32.750
But until that happens-- so that's one technology
that's coming.

00:57:32.750 --> 00:57:34.780
You might want to look for it.

00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:38.160
The other way to do it is actually to use
the semi-structured.

00:57:38.160 --> 00:57:45.960
And all I've heard is just look out.

00:57:45.960 --> 00:57:53.980
I think you'll find increasingly more vendors
providing more support for semi-structured

00:57:53.980 --> 00:57:54.980
data.

00:57:54.980 --> 00:57:58.240
So in a semi-structured world, you can certainly
have a structured part of it.

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:01.070
You can just say, well, that part of it is
fixed, right?

00:58:01.070 --> 00:58:03.210
And this other part is variable.

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:06.700
So you could use a semi-structured model to
do that.

00:58:06.700 --> 00:58:07.700
OK.

00:58:07.700 --> 00:58:12.880
So I think we have time for one more question,
which is, so one of the interesting things,

00:58:12.880 --> 00:58:17.849
at least in the biomedical space, is about
scientific reproducibility.

00:58:17.849 --> 00:58:24.460
And could you speak to the provenance of the
data, particularly in terms of the translational

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:30.109
data processes that a database may support?

00:58:30.109 --> 00:58:31.269
Yes.

00:58:31.269 --> 00:58:41.119
And I actually think a lot of work has gone
on in computer science research about this

00:58:41.119 --> 00:58:42.440
notion of provenance.

00:58:42.440 --> 00:58:48.099
By the way, I think there are even-- at least
workshop, I don't think conference-- that

00:58:48.099 --> 00:58:50.759
I've talked about provenance big data.

00:58:50.759 --> 00:58:55.180
But as you can imagine, though, not only do
you have this little itty-bitty piece of data

00:58:55.180 --> 00:59:00.359
that you care about, but if you kept its provenance,
the provenance can be huge, compared to the

00:59:00.359 --> 00:59:01.450
data that you have.

00:59:01.450 --> 00:59:05.670
So people are already starting to worry about,
what's the explosion that's going to happen

00:59:05.670 --> 00:59:09.990
in the amount of data to start keeping the
provenance of it.

00:59:09.990 --> 00:59:17.750
What we don't have yet, I believe, is good
systems, software systems or products that

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:18.750
do a good job.

00:59:18.750 --> 00:59:23.560
I mean, there are certainly products that
are trying to help in that way.

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:25.920
I don't think it's zero.

00:59:25.920 --> 00:59:29.599
But I think that part of it is just beginning
to happen.

00:59:29.599 --> 00:59:40.250
So I think something a lot more systematic
in software systems need to be available so

00:59:40.250 --> 00:59:48.500
that every one of us could be using provenance
with our data.

00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:55.700
I'm just thinking about, in the computer science
world, there is a lot of research.

00:59:55.700 --> 00:59:56.700
Here's one example.

00:59:56.700 --> 01:00:01.660
So even when you run an SQL query, a very
structured database-- SQL is a structured

01:00:01.660 --> 01:00:04.619
language-- you get a result back.

01:00:04.619 --> 01:00:08.540
And for those of you who are familiar with
SQL, you have joins, right?

01:00:08.540 --> 01:00:12.200
So I'm going to have lots of joins going on
and things like this.

01:00:12.200 --> 01:00:17.200
And you get a result back, and a lot of times,
you might want to say, well, how did this

01:00:17.200 --> 01:00:19.900
particular row appear in this result set?

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:21.359
I wasn't expecting it.

01:00:21.359 --> 01:00:27.930
So people are actually looking at actually
the provenance of individual rows in the query

01:00:27.930 --> 01:00:31.759
of a relational database, which actually itself
turns out to be quite an interesting challenging

01:00:31.759 --> 01:00:32.759
problem.

01:00:32.759 --> 01:00:36.920
Because you really should be able to click
on it and say, tell me how this came in.

01:00:36.920 --> 01:00:40.400
And the system should be able to say, well,
this came in because I joined that with this,

01:00:40.400 --> 01:00:43.280
and we selected this one over there, and there's
[INAUDIBLE] up here.

01:00:43.280 --> 01:00:48.880
So these are all beginning to happen, and
there's just going to be even more data.

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:55.849
And also, there's a recent paper somebody
just sent me about why all of this is actually

01:00:55.849 --> 01:01:01.270
going to end up becoming a big computational
burden, as well.

01:01:01.270 --> 01:01:02.270
OK.

01:01:02.270 --> 01:01:03.890
So I think we're out of time.

01:01:03.890 --> 01:01:06.480
So thank you again so much for this wonderful
talk.

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:11.460
We're getting a lot of positive feedback already
from the attendees, and we'll end it here.

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:12.460
Thank you.

01:01:12.460 --> 01:01:13.460
All right.

01:01:13.460 --> 01:01:14.460
Great.

01:01:14.460 --> 01:01:15.460
Thanks.

01:01:15.460 --> 01:01:15.800
Very glad to be with you.

